<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Hudson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1565–<a href="/years/1611">1611</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Hudson was an English navigator who searched for a northern sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Sailing for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> in the Half Moon in 1609, he explored the river now named after him, opening the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> and New Netherland. On a voyage for English backers in 1610–1611 he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a>, where his crew mutinied and set him adrift; he was never seen again.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-hudson.jpg" alt="Henry Hudson" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HenryHudson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Almost nothing is known for certain of Hudson's early life. His year of birth is variously estimated between 1560 and 1570, and he may have been born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. When he first appears in the record in 1607 he was already an experienced mariner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1607 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company">Muscovy Company</a> hired Hudson to find a northerly route to the Pacific coast of Asia, in the belief that the summer sun would melt the ice. He sailed in the Hopewell, reached Spitsbergen and turned back when ice blocked the north coast. In 1608 he tried the route east around northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> but found the ice impenetrable at Novaya Zemlya.</p><p>In 1609 merchants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> chose him to find an eastern passage. Ice stopped the Halve Maen east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s North Cape, and Hudson, acting outside his instructions, turned west. After landfall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and a landing in which a dozen of his men attacked a village, he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod">Cape Cod</a> and the coast to the north. On 3 September he came to the estuary of the river now named for him. Verrazzano had known it since 1524. Hudson sailed up the river for ten days, buying oysters and beans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> in canoes and trading for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>.</p><p>In 1610, backed by the Virginia Company and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, Hudson sailed the Discovery. He passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, entered Hudson Strait on 25 June and, on 2 August, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a>. Ice trapped the ship in James Bay in November and the crew wintered ashore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the ice cleared in spring 1611 Hudson meant to press on exploring, but most of the crew wished to go home and mutinied in June. Hudson, his teenage son John and seven others were set adrift in an open shallop and were never seen by Europeans again; later searches, among them Thomas Button's in 1612, found nothing. The main source, the account of Abacuk Pricket, blames Henry Greene and Robert Juet, and historians have doubted it, since the survivors faced trial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the men blamed were dead. Some survivors were tried, but none was punished.</p><p>Hudson's 1609 voyage established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> claims to the region and to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>; a trading post stood at Albany in 1614, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> became the capital of New Netherland in 1625. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> later gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> access to a fur trade along its shores for more than two centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Hudson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1609 the English captain <b>Henry Hudson</b>, hired by the Dutch East India Company to find a passage to Asia, sailed the flyboat Halve Maen into Upper New York Bay and up the river now named for him until the water grew too shallow at the site of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
